In this lesson, we'll look at simplifying linear expressions by combining like terms. A linear expression just means each variable is raised to the first power, so something like three x plus seven, or two a plus four b minus nine. No x squared, no square roots, nothing fancy.

Like terms are terms that share the exact same variable part. So three x and five x are like terms, and you can combine them to get eight x. Two y minus seven y gives you minus five y. But three x and two y are not like terms, because the variables don't match, so you leave them alone.

When you simplify, you are really just using a few properties. The commutative property lets you reorder terms, so three x plus five x plus two is the same as five x plus three x plus two. The associative property lets you regroup them, so you can bring all the variable terms together and all the constant terms together first. Then you simply combine what matches. Adding zero changes nothing, and a term minus itself cancels to zero. If there are brackets, expand them first using the distributive law before you start combining.

